    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so in the AC one, because it's constantly charging and discharging, does that mean the capacitor isn't actually \"blocking\" the current like it does in DC? it feels like there's some kind of movement happening even though nothing is physically flowing through the dielectric. can u clarify that part?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely correct; the capacitor never \"blocks\" current in a steady AC circuit in the same way it does in DC. Although no electrons physically cross the dielectric gap to create a short circuit, a changing electric field within the capacitor generates a displacement current that effectively acts as if current were flowing continuously. This dynamic process means charge oscillates between the plates at every half-cycle of the AC signal, which is exactly why the formula for capacitive reactance includes the frequency term to calculate that effortless opposition.",
